In C, a **file** is simply a named stream of data that resides outside your program’s memory—usually on a hard drive, SSD, USB stick, network share, or even a virtual device (like `/dev/null`).  
C treats it as an opaque object that you manipulate through the standard I/O library (`stdio.h`), which provides a set of functions that work with a `FILE *` pointer.

---

## 1. Opening a File

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    /* fopen returns a pointer to a FILE object or NULL on failure */
    FILE *fp = fopen("example.txt", "r");   /* open for reading */
    if (!fp) {
        perror("fopen");
        return 1;
    }

    /* … use fp … */

    fclose(fp);          /* close when finished */
    return 0;
}
```

| Mode string | Meaning |
|-------------|---------|
| `"r"`       | Read text (fails if file doesn’t exist) |
| `"w"`       | Write text (creates/truncates) |
| `"a"`       | Append text (creates if missing) |
| `"rb"`      | Binary read |
| `"wb"`      | Binary write |
| `"ab"`      | Binary append |
| `"r+"`      | Read/write text |
| `"w+"`      | Read/write text (truncate) |
| `"a+"`      | Read/append text |
| `"rb+"`     | Read/write binary |
| `"wb+"`     | Read/write binary (truncate) |
| `"ab+"`     | Read/append binary |

> **Tip:** On Windows, always use the binary modes (`b`) if you’re dealing with non‑text data; otherwise line endings may get translated automatically.

---

## 2. Reading & Writing

### Text Mode

```c
char buffer[256];
while (fgets(buffer, sizeof buffer, fp)) {   /* reads until newline or EOF */
    printf("%s", buffer);
}
```

### Binary Mode

```c
unsigned char buf[128];
size_t nread = fread(buf, 1, sizeof buf, fp);   /* reads raw bytes */
if (nread == 0 && feof(fp)) {
    /* reached end of file */
}
```

Writing is analogous:

```c
fprintf(fp, "Hello, world!\n");   /* formatted text */
fwrite(data, 1, len, fp);         /* raw bytes */
```

---

## 3. Seeking & Positioning

```c
/* Move to byte offset 100 from start */
fseek(fp, 100, SEEK_SET);

/* Move 20 bytes forward from current position */
fseek(fp, 20, SEEK_CUR);

/* Move to 30 bytes before end */
fseek(fp, -30, SEEK_END);
```

After seeking, you can query the new position:

```c
long pos = ftell(fp);   /* returns current offset or -1 on error */
```

---

## 4. Error Handling

All I/O functions return values that indicate success or failure. Common patterns:

```c
if (ferror(fp)) {
    perror("I/O error");
}
```

Use `feof(fp)` to detect end‑of‑file after a read attempt that returned fewer items than requested.

---

## 5. Closing

Always close a file when you’re done:

```c
if (fclose(fp) != 0) {
    perror("fclose");
}
```

If you forget to close, the OS will eventually reclaim the resources, but you’ll lose any buffered output and risk data corruption.

---

## 6. Example: Copying a File

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc != 3) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s src dst\n", argv[0]);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    FILE *src = fopen(argv[1], "rb");
    FILE *dst = fopen(argv[2], "wb");

    if (!src || !dst) {
        perror("fopen");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    unsigned char buf[4096];
    size_t n;
    while ((n = fread(buf, 1, sizeof buf, src)) > 0) {
        if (fwrite(buf, 1, n, dst) != n) {
            perror("fwrite");
            break;
        }
    }

    if (ferror(src)) perror("fread");

    fclose(src);
    fclose(dst);

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

---

## 7. Summary

| Concept | Key Functions | Typical Use |
|---------|---------------|-------------|
| Open | `fopen`, `freopen` | Get a `FILE *` |
| Close | `fclose` | Release resources |
| Read | `fgetc`, `fgets`, `fread` | Pull data into buffers |
| Write | `fputc`, `fprintf`, `fwrite` | Push data out |
| Seek | `fseek`, `ftell` | Reposition cursor |
| Error | `ferror`, `perror` | Diagnose problems |

That’s the core of file handling in C. Once you master these primitives, you can build higher‑level abstractions (e.g., buffered readers, custom file formats, or even simple databases).
